## With real data



The main example, [vocab_assessment_3_to_8_year_old_children](https://itemresponsewarehouse.org/tables/vocab_assessment_3_to_8_year_old_children/), tests a theory of response behaviour, standing in for the balance scale, which the IRW doesn't hold. The contrast, [robison_2026_retesting_stroop](https://itemresponsewarehouse.org/tables/robison_2026_retesting_stroop/), has a cause that works in everyone and asks whether it ranks anyone. [Download the code](code/validity-causal-irw.R); it needs only base R.

### A vocabulary task with a theory

oREV is a receptive vocabulary task for German-speaking children aged 3 to 8 ([Bohn et al., 2022](https://doi.org/10.31234/osf.io/4z86w)). A child hears a word and picks one of four pictures: the target, a semantically related distractor, a phonologically similar one and an unrelated one. Twenty of the 52 words were added to an existing task for their rated ages of acquisition, between six and ten years (pp. 8–9). A response is 1 when the child picked the target, and nothing is missing. The `item` column is the trial position, which holds different words in the two presentation orders, so the word (`targetword`) is the item here.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="show"}
